  "articleBody": "Choosing the right paint for your bathroom comes down to moisture resistance and long-term durability. While standard interior wall paint works fine in bedrooms, bathrooms face extreme daily humidity and shower steam.\n\nKey Differences\nStandard eggshell or satin interior paints rely on basic binders that can soften under prolonged steam, leading to moisture blisters, water tracks, and mildew growth. Purpose-engineered bathroom paints, such as Benjamin Moore's Bath & Spa, feature specialized mildew-resistant additives and advanced resin technology that seal the substrate even in low-luster or matte finishes.\n\nWhen to Choose Each\nIf you are painting a well-ventilated powder room with no shower, a standard high-quality satin or semi-gloss paint is usually sufficient. But for full baths and master suites with daily showers, investing in a dedicated bathroom coating prevents recurring peel and stain marks. \n\nTimeline and Service Curing\nStandard latex paints can often take light contact quickly, but technical data sheets for premium bathroom coatings like Bath & Spa advise waiting at least 24 hours before subjecting new paint to full shower moisture.\n\nWhat to Do Next\nIf your existing bathroom paint is already peeling or bubbling, painting over it won't fix underlying moisture traps. HH Remodeling LLC handles complete bathroom surface preparation, drywall repairs, and interior painting throughout the Aurora and Denver areas. Call owner Abraham Becerra at 303-505-0866 to discuss your finish plans.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
